      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine how many integers Josef can pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer when dividing 1000 by that integer, we need to find the divisors of 1000. The divisors of a number \\( n \\) are the integers that divide \\( n \\) without leaving a remainder.\n\nLet's list all the divisors of 1000. First, we can factorize 1000 into its prime factors:\n\\[ 1000 = 10^3 = (2 \\cdot 5)^3 = 2^3 \\cdot 5^3 \\]\n\nUsing the prime factorization, we can generate the divisors by taking all possible combinations of the prime factors:\n\n\\[ 2^0 \\cdot 5^0 \\]\n\\[ 2^1 \\cdot 5^0 \\]\n\\[ 2^2 \\cdot 5^0 \\]\n\\[ 2^3 \\cdot 5^0 \\]\n\\[ 2^0 \\cdot 5^1 \\]\n\\[ 2^1 \\cdot 5^1 \\]\n\\[ 2^2 \\cdot 5^1 \\]\n\\[ 2^3 \\cdot 5^1 \\]\n\\[ 2^0 \\cdot 5^2 \\]\n\\[ 2^1 \\cdot 5^2 \\]\n\\[ 2^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\]\n\\[ 2^3 \\cdot 5^2 \\]\n\\[ 2^0 \\cdot 5^3 \\]\n\\[ 2^1 \\cdot 5^3 \\]\n\\[ 2^2 \\cdot 5^3 \\]\n\\[ 2^3 \\cdot 5^3 \\]\n\nSimplifying, the divisors of 1000 are:\n\\[ 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000 \\]\n\nCounting these divisors, we find there are 16 divisors. Thus, Josef can pick any number from the list:\n\\[ 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000 \\]\n\nSo, the number of integers Josef can pick is \\( \\boxed{16} \\).",
