      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine how many integers Josef can pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer, we need to understand when \\( \\frac{1000}{n} \\) is an integer. This happens if and only if \\( n \\) is a divisor of 1000. \n\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of 1000:\n\\[\n1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3\n\\]\nGiven the prime factorization, the number of divisors of 1000 can be determined by using the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of divisors} = (3+1)(3+1) = 16\n\\]\nThis reflects the total number of ways to distribute the prime factors among the divisors.\n\nWe denote divisors of 1000 as \\( n \\), and the set of all such divisors will thus be \\( D = \\{1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000\\} \\).\n\nNow, we verify which of these numbers \\( n \\) divide \\( 1000 \\). We will use Python to identify these set members ensuring precise checks.\n```python\n# Finding all divisors of 1000.\ntarget = 1000\nmy_divisors = [i for i in range(1, target + 1) if target % i == 0]\n\n# Displaying such divisors\nprint((my_divisors, len(my_divisors)))\n```\n```output\n([1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000], 16)\n```\nBy checking the typical divisibility checks, we've found out that the distinct divisors of 1000 are:\n\n\\[\n[1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000]\n\\]\n\nThis set of divisors is:\n\n\\[\n16\\ \\text{divisor(s) per bit in 1000's prime factors:} \n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of integers that Josef could pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
